\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- Dollar stands tall amid Asia turmoil. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 6 (Reuters) - The dollar held its own against the Japanese yen on Tuesday despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it continued to climb versus other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid persistent pessimism about Asia's economies. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had only a brief impact, as demand for the greenback from Japanese importers and institutional investors overwhelmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to support the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to 134.38 yen earlier on Tuesday, reaching its highest level since April 1992, before the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  knocked it down to the morning's low of 132.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is believed to have stepped in to sell dollars at around 134.20 yen. It later sold dollars intermittently below 134 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar briefly fell below 133 yen after BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the dollar quickly recovered by about one yen from the low as Japanese importers and life insurers bought dollars aggressively," said a manager at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  "More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only result in more of the same. Dollar demand is just overwhelming," he added. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka expressing concerns about the yen's weakness were largely ignored by the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There is no doubt that we are worried. Excessive weakness of the yen is not favourable," Mitsuzuka told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at around 133.40/50 yen as of 0300 GMT, compared with Monday's New York close of 133.48/58 yen. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was of no help to the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weak across the board. The dollar's safe-haven status remains intact as there is a strong 'sell Asia' mood," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah fell to a new low of 7,600 to the dollar on Tuesday, after opening at 7,000/7,200. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso fell, breaching the first volatility band of 44.356 to the dollar, compared with the previous close of 42.65. The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell $1.5 million at the rate of 44.35 pesos. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also plunged to a new low of 52.00 to the dollar in the onshore market, compared with 50.00/50.20 late on Monday. It fell to 52.00/52.20 in the offshore market from 49.85/50.35 on late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won was also slightly weaker. The won stood around 1,805.0/1,810.0, compared with Monday's close of 1,780. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was also dragged lower on weakness of the yen and Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Aussie briefly fell to around US$0.6352, its lowest level since December 1996, compared with US$0.6480/85 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Pessimism about Asia's economic health was also reflected in other financial markets in Japan. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 stock average ended Tuesday's morning session down 127.17 points or 0.85 percent at 14,829.67. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 182nd JGB yield stood at 1.590 percent at midday, down from Monday's close of 1.610 percent. \par \par \uc2  At one point, the yield dipped as low as 1.575 percent, just above its record low of 1.565 percent hit on December 5, 1997. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, yen-based gold futures were lower at the end of Tuesday morning amid a bearish mood carried over from a slump in gold prices to new 18-year lows overnight, traders said. "Sentiment is bearish, and prices are expected to fall towards $275 in the near term," one brokerage analyst said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries traded in Tokyo were capped by light profit-taking and corrective sales after the 6.125 percent 30-year bond hit a record low yield in New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  Traders in New York were heartened after comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan suggesting that the U.S. economy may face deflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-9780 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du16015v6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
