\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Rubin Inspires Mark-Selling For Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 1998\par 22:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The Bank of International Settlements reported early Monday that German, U.K. and French banks increased their exposure to Asia in the six months before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis began, while U.S. lending to the region dropped slightly. \par \par \uc2  'The European economies do have more exposure to Asia than their leaders have been letting on and this provides a backdrop as to why the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now getting caught up in Asia's problems,' said Stephen Flanagan, chief dealer with Erste Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Flanagan said a break below key support levels against the dollar late last year in sterling and the Swiss franc - the preferred safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for much of 1997 - helped 'set up a broad-based dollar rally' for 1998. \par \par \uc2  After it touched Y134.00, its highest level since April 27, 1992, the dollar eased slightly against the yen in afternoon New York trade but continued to rise against the mark. Traders said this reflected fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday to defend the yen. \par \par \uc2  Erste Bank's Flanagan said this speculation was helped by comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, who told reporters midday in New York that he shares Japanese concerns about the yen 'weakening too substantially.' \par \par \uc2  Rubin also reiterated that a strong dollar is in U.S. interests. The two statements, Flanagan said, sent traders a combined signal to sell marks for dollars and yen. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the mark was at Y73.24, down from an intraday high of Y73.71. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also driven to cautious, BOJ-wary yen positions late in New York by the earlier hawkish comments of Haruhiko Kuroda, head of the international finance bureau at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kuroda said he 'doesn't accept a further weakening of the yen.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 05-01-98 \par \par \uc2  2154GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 1865 20714 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du15001gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
