\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dollar Seen Edging Higher Vs DEM, Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Podolsky and Michael Casey \par \uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 1998\par 00:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Against a backdrop of uncertainty over Asian financial turmoil and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union, the draw of the dollar's security will continue this week. \par \par \uc2  When full-scale global trading resumes Monday after the traditional year-end lull, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to prolong its advance against the yen and the Deutsche mark as traders establish new positions. \par \par \uc2  In thin trading Friday, with Tokyo markets closed and many major European and U.S. participants still sidelined by the holiday break, the dollar rose to five-and-a-half year highs against the yen and four-month highs against the mark. The dollar hit an intraday high Friday of Y132.46, its peak since May, 1992. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the dollar was quoted at DM1.8045, up from DM1.7980 late Wednesday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also quoted at Y132.40, up from Y130.57. Sterling was trading at $1.6407, down from $1.6508 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  However, the further the dollar climbs above Y130.00 and DM1.8000, the more dollar bullishness will be tempered by the threat Japanese or German authorities will take steps to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But these concerns don't mute a fundamental theme: despite expectations U.S. growth will slow in 1998, U.S. assets remain attractive, burnishing the dollar's luster. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 56.79 points Friday after finishing 1997 22.64% higher than its 1996 close. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders viewed several indicators last week as providing evidence the U.S. economy is healthy. \par \par \uc2  Consumer confidence hit a 28-high, according to last week's Conference Board report, while higher-than-expected jobless claims for the week ended Dec. 27 eased fears a tightening labor market will create inflationary pressures. Manufacturing activity slowed in December, according to the National Association of Purchasing Management. \par \par \uc2  In Chicago Saturday, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan cautioned about the negative impact of 'very rapid asset price declines,' but his comments are unlikely to move the dollar. The subject of Greenspan's speech was the accurate measure of inflation. \par \par \uc2  While the strong economic data suggests the dollar should climb firmly against the yen, the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is real. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's influential vice minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara bluntly warned that the BOJ will 'take decisive steps' to defend the yen and that it would '\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such a way that it will surprise the markets.' \par \par \uc2  The BOJ already spent $10 billion late in December to defend the yen, notes David Durrant, New York-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the research firm I.D.E.A., temporarily driving the dollar sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 04-01-98 \par \par \uc2  2300GMT \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1400201\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
