\par\par \b \uc2  =Fed Data Hint At Large U.S. T-Bill Sale By Bank of Japan \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 1997\par 00:06\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Analysts say the Bank of Japan would be the logical party behind another recent sale of U.S. Treasury securities evident in Federal Reserve data. \par \par \uc2  Friday, the Federal Reserve Bank of New York reported an outright purchase of T-bills from a foreign holder and a huge, $12.5-billion drop in custody holdings. \par \par \uc2  The figures showed that a "customer," - Fed parlance for a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or international agency - sold $2 billion outright in T-bills to the Fed. \par \par \uc2  This is only the third time this year a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made a direct sale of Treasury securities to the Fed. The last time such a transaction occurred was during the week ended Dec. 10, when the Fed bought $1 billion outright from a foreign official institution. \par \par \uc2  In the week ended Dec. 24, U.S. Treasury securities held by the Fed on behalf of foreign official institutions, fell $12.474 billion to $603.376 billion. \par \par \uc2  Lou Crandall, chief economist at R.H. Wrightson & Associates, said Japan was "almost certainly" played a part in the outright T-bill sale to the Fed and the large drop in custody holdings. "They released funds to (South) Korea, which would require them to liquidate Treasurys. And they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, which would require them to liquidate Treasurys. The bill sector got flooded, and since Japan is the single biggest player in the bill sector. It fits nicely," Crandall noted. \par \par \uc2  Analysts pointed out that the $2 billion outright sale to the Fed and the unusually large drop in custody holdings coincide with the period during which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was known to be aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, supporting the yen through dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ most likely financed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with its dollar reserves, which are normally parked in short-term Treasury securities, they said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  12-29-97 \par \par \uc2  1806EST \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtct02shk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
