\par\par \b \uc2  Official Says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Ready To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Markets To Defend Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 1997\par 23:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to take decisive steps to prevent excessive depreciation of the yen and is ready to surprise investors with further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary, a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, vice minister for international affairs, on a television talk show, said Japan "will take decisive steps to fend off excessive yen weakness." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , operating on the ministry's instructions, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in mid-December but didn't enter the market as the yen fell sharply last week. Some traders are nervous that Japanese monetary authorities will take advantage of thin end-of-the-year market conditions to push the dollar lower against the yen this week. \par \par \uc2  Regarding Japan's troubled financial system, Sakikabara emphasized that he wants to avoid allowing any more Japanese banks to fail. "If there are problematic places, we should encourage restructuring, or buyouts or replacing management, but we can't allow failures," he said. \par \par \uc2  As for Hokkaido Takushoku Bank, which failed last month, Sakakibara said, "We shouldn't have let it go bust." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara voiced confidence that the government's plans to use public funds will be enough to restore faith in the country's financial system. \par \par \uc2  The government has announced plans that would give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to up to 30 trillion yen ($230 billion) in funds aimed at stabilizing the financial system. \par \par \uc2  "If we have 30 trillion yen, that should be enough," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  Turning to the situation in South Korea, Sakakibara suggested plans are in the works to help calm turmoil in Seoul financial markets. \par \par \uc2  "Monday in New York, Japan, the U.S. and Europe will take steps to ease the credit crunch in South Korea," Sakakibara said. It was unclear if the vice minister was referring to new steps or was simply saying that a plan announced Dec. 24 for an early disbursement of financial aid from Group of Seven countries (G-7) would be implemented Monday. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the G-7 released a joint statement saying they would accelerate the schedule for their aid provisions to South Korea, disbursing a total of $8 billion by early January. In addition, the International Monetary Fund said that on Dec. 30 it would disburse $2 billion of the $57 billion rescue package it unveiled for South Korea earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Some market watchers feel the IMF's plan to speed the South Korean rescue package also could help Japan, which has seen its own financial woes magnified by those of its neighbor. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtcs01740\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
