\par\par \b \uc2  Sakakibara: Ready To Surprise Mkts With More  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 1997\par 22:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to take decisive steps to prevent excessive depreciation of the yen and is ready to surprise the markets through further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, vice minister for international affairs, said during a television talk show, 'We will take decisive steps to fend off excessive yen weakness. To that end, we've already poured our utmost efforts. And we are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such a way that it will surprise the markets from now as well.' \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , operating on the ministry's instructions, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in mid-December but didn't enter the market last week. Some traders are nervous that Japanese monetary authorities will take advantage of thin end-of-the-year market conditions to push the dollar lower against the yen this week. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 28-12-97 \par \par \uc2  2100GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtcs09wz8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
