\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up vs yen in Tokyo after bank downgrading. \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 25 (Reuters) - ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACTIVITY: \par \par \uc2  0010 GMT -- \par \par \uc2  *Dollar edged higher versus yen in early Tokyo trade, as yen remained under pressure following overnight news of downgrading of Japanese banks. \par \par \uc2  *But activity extremely thin due to Christmas, with very few players willing to trade actively, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Market virtually ignored Japan's preliminary industrial output data for November and customs-cleared trade data for first 10-days of December. \par \par \uc2  *Japan's industrial production fell a preliminary 4.1 percent in November from previous month. Economists' forecasts ranged from fall of 1.9 percent to fall of 3.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  *"Output data was weaker-than-expected, but there was little market reaction as nobody is trading actively this morning," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  *Japan's customs-cleared trade surplus rose 1,655.1 percent to 107.29 billion yen in first 10 days of December compared with a revised surplus of 6.11 billion yen in same period a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  *"Dollar is slightly higher probably due to downgrading of Japanese banks overnight. But it will probably remain between 129.80 yen to 130.20 yen today," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  *Standard & Poor's Corp on Wednesday downgraded Japan's Sanwa Bank Ltd and Sakura Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  *S&P also said it may cut ratings of four other Japanese banks, Fuji Bank Ltd, Industrial Bank of Japan, Tokai Bank Ltd and Asahi Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar demand related to setting up of fresh investment trust funds also helped to push greenback higher ahead of 0100 GMT Tokyo fixing, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Meanwhile, overnight news that International Monetary Fund (IMF) and other countries would provide $10 billion in aid to South Korea ahead of schedule was positive for yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Japanese monetary authorities were likely to remain eager to check dollar's rise, but Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday due to thin volume, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *"Most people doubt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars at a time when number of market participants is limited. Authorities should prefer to wait for dollar longs to build up," said the trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  *BOJ is believed to have spent total of around $10 billion last week in effort to halt dollar's rise versus yen, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been detected in market so far this week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar moved narrowly versus mark in early Tokyo. Dealers said dollar/mark was unlikely to move out of 1.7700 to 1.7750 mark range as European and U.S. markets closed. \par \par \uc2  *Dollar closed at 129.65/75 yen and 1.7720/30 marks in New York on Wednesday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcp00oh3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
