\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seen at 135 yen without joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Risa Maeda \par \uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 1997\par 11:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 19 (Reuters) - The dollar is likely to climb to as high as 135 yen without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of U.S. and European central banks, Tokyo dealers say. "Doubts are growing about the effectiveness of this week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The sole way to put off such doubt would be joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Another collapse by a Japanese company overshadowed the prospects of the yen, keeping away buyers with the exception of the Bank of Japan (BOJ), which aggressively sold dollars for yen to defend the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But dealers said BOJ sales alone would not be enough. \par \par \uc2  "If (global) authorities fail to let markets know that they would jointly protect the yen against a fall under 130 to the dollar, it could break through this year's low of 131.60 and towards 132-133 in thin year-end trading," said Hideki Naito, managing analyst at MMS International. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expressed doubts about the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. Federal Reserve or European central banks, adding that it might prove futile anyway in the face of fundamentals favouring the yen's deterioration. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's failure of Japanese foodstuffs trader Toshoku Ltd sent shockwaves through Tokyo financial markets on Friday, causing a fall of more than five percent in the key Nikkei average of 225 leading shares and making banks' fund-raising difficult in the interbank money market. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that other firms could follow Toshoku due to stricter bank lending policies hurt shares of several companies, including trading firm Tomen Corp and apparel company Renown Inc. \par \par \uc2  If more companies fail, increasing the amount of problem loans, banks would in turn tighten their lending stance further and hinder Japan's economic recovery, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The failure of Toshoku put fire to live coals. The stability of Japan's financial system is again in doubt," said the U.S. bank senior manager. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said buying by U.S. operators and domestic investors overwhelmed the repeated and massive dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ on Friday -- the third straight day of real and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese and U.S. officials. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters on Friday that the United States and Japan share concerns about the excessive weakness in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Japan and the United States have shared concerns against the excessive weakness in the yen," said Sakakibara, know as "Mr Yen" for his sway over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  His comments came after a U.S. Treasury official said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was appropriate and consistent with U.S. concerns about the sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the dollar remained undeterred on Friday by both words and selling, dealers said. The BOJ spent between $4 billion and $5 billion defending the yen on Friday, surpassing the estimated $3 billion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used on Wednesday, they said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback stood at about 128.72/75 yen in late trade, compared with 128.66/76 yen in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar fell to 125.70 yen on the surprise effect of BOJ's dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the first such action since August 1992 -- coupled with the announcement of the government's income tax cut. \par \par \uc2  "The market realised the yen would remain vulnerable, with or without the two trillion yen tax cut, after seeing steep falls in Tokyo stocks after the failure of Toshoku. So any dips of the dollar provided a good bargain-hunting opportunity," said Taro Ogai, manager at the international treasury department of the Industrial Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  MMS's Naito said the BOJ's reluctance to pull the dollar below 125 yen on Wednesday was a mistake. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ stopped selling when the dollar approached 125 yen. That gave dollar bulls relief because they thought the Japanese authorities were worried that the dollar's fall to 120 yen would hurt exporters' profits," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exports, which become more competitive when the yen is weaker against the dollar, have been the brightest spot in Japan's stagnant economy. ((Tokyo Treasury desk, +813 3 432-8570 fax +813 3 432-7462, tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcj00rki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
