\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan risks ending year as it began--badly. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 1997\par 08:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 19 (Reuters) - Japan looks to be ending the year as it began it -- badly. \par \par \uc2  Share prices plunged and sentiment towards the yen remained depressed despite dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy and financial system persisted. \par \par \uc2  Not even a fiscal policy U-turn in the form of a surprise income tax cut earlier in the week could provide lasting cheer. \par \par \uc2  It was a gloomy re-run of the start of 1997, when the stock market sagged and the yen sank on fears -- later realised -- that a fiscal austerity regime aimed at curbing the budget deficit would scuttle Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  "It's still a very vulnerable situation and some people close to the prime minister think that all the (policy) bullets have been shot and there are none left,` said Takashi Kiuchi, chief economist at LTCB Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  The Tokyo stock market's main barometer, the 225-share Nikkei average, plummeted perilously close to the key 15,000 level before closing down 846.75 points or 5.24 percent at 15,314.89. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said sentiment was battered by the failure on Thursday of medium-sized foodstuffs trader Toshoku Ltd and by a triple-digit loss in New York stocks on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear Toshoku's collapse -- the ninth failure of a listed firm this year -- reflects a deepening credit crunch as Japan's bad loan-laden banks tighten lending ahead of the introduction of stricter capital adequacy standards and planned financial deregulation. \par \par \uc2  The share price plunge, which added to Thursday's losses, came two days after Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced a two trillion yen ($15.5 billion) income tax rebate in a surprise easing of his tight fiscal stance. \par \par \uc2  The rebates were included in a ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) package of economic proposals which officials said was worth about five trillion yen in total. \par \par \uc2  The LDP also unveiled this week a batch of measures aimed at stabilising the financial system, including a plan for the government to contribute 10 trillion yen worth of bonds to the Deposit Insurance Corp (DIC) to protect depositors and help boost capital at troubled financial firms. \par \par \uc2  Government officials insist that while growth this business year, ending next March, will be flat, the measures taken will both prevent future financial failures and help the economy to grow by close to two percent in 1998/99. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet was expected to approve later on Friday government forecasts of 0.1 percent growth in gross domestic product (GDP) in 1997/98 and 1.9 percent the following year. \par \par \uc2  But private economists said the economy -- already seen by some as likely to contract this quarter and next -- would continue to slump if no additional policy support was provided. \par \par \uc2  "It will be a miracle if we get much positive growth in the final quarter of 1997 and the first quarter of 1998," said Russell Jones, chief economist at Lehman Brothers. "Flat to down is our view, and it's a moot point how the economy performs after that. Much depends on the long-term response." \par \par \uc2  Some analysts hope that Hashimoto's reluctant policy shift on the income tax cuts, which the premier insists was not a violation of his fiscal reform pledge, has opened the door to further pump-priming measures. "The LDP is normally a very pragmatic party and that means in terms of policy options they tend to keep every option open," LTCB's Kiuchi said. \par \par \uc2  Others said, however, that Hashimoto may have to quit to clear the way for bolder policy measures. \par \par \uc2  "It is difficult to see how this government can go much further without actually having to resign," Jones said. "This government has probably shot its bolt." \par \par \uc2  Economists expect more corporate failures both inside and outside of the financial sector, which itself is plagued by bad loans and high funding costs as creditors boost lending rates to offset risk. \par \par \uc2  "January-March is a very dangerous period, because when the business year ends, many people have to decide if they will survive or give up," Kiuchi said. \par \par \uc2  "It may include not only financial institutions but other companies like construction or other industrial firms." \par \par \uc2  Given the gloomy outlook, private economists said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at bolstering the yen and avoiding trade friction with the United States was not only ineffective but also misguided. "The last thing they should be doing is trying to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Jones said. \par \par \uc2  Economists also dismissed vague talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might cut its official discount rate, already stuck at a record low of 0.5 percent for more than two years. "Rates are nil already and a cut would signal panic," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at JP Morgan in Tokyo. ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcj00rk3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
