\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen bears gain ground in late trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 19 (Reuters) - Yen bears started to gain the upper hand at the end of a volatile week in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, mindful of the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the yen but putting more store into the bleak economic fundamental outlook for Japan. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen circled the 129 level in afternoon Europe, a level the Bank of Japan pushed it below in its third round of dollar/yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Asia session on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be inevitable if we get close to 130 again, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is beginning to lose its impact and the weak yen trend is still there," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei stock index slump overnight on the back a large corporate failure saw the gloom hanging over the Japan economy settle in again after being lifted slightly earlier in the week by an unexpectedly generous fiscal package announced by the Japanese government. \par \par \uc2  But analysts' reassessment of the economic stimulus package has raised doubts of its effectiveness in turning Japan's economy around and saw the yen gains made in the middle of this week being whittled away. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen stood at 128.92/9.02 at 1550 GMT, up from a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  low today of 128.28 and 128.70/75 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised speculation that other central banks in Europe and the U.S. may also join in propping the yen, but dealers in London said they did not expect that to happen this week. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  a lot, but the market looks unimpressed," said Klaus Kusber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. "But if, for example, the Bundesbank and the U.S. were seen helping, that would impress the market much more." \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen was inching back up to 73 from a session low of 72.44, and the yen also lost more ground to sterling. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/yen stood at 214.95/5.51 in late Europe, back to levels seen before the Bank of Japan started selling dollars on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/yen demand helped buoy sterling across the board, posting gains against the dollar and testing earlier highs against the mark. Easing concerns that UK intersest rates had peaked continued to support the pound. Sterling focus next week will be UK GDP and trade data. \par \par \uc2  The pound was trading at $1.6718/23 from $1.6632/37 late on Thursday, but sterling/mark was down on a late dollar/mark retreat, trading at 2.9518/27 from a session high of 2.9635. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being, I think sterling will hold up relatively well," said Nick Stamenkovic, economist at DKB International. "But if we see signs of the UK economy losing momentum, then sterling will inevitably weaken." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark's late dip came amid a more than 200-point drop in the Dow Jones Industrial Average, and dealers cited some safe-haven flows into marks and Swiss francs. U.S. stock markets were said to be down on worries over the impact of Asia on U.S. corporate earnings. \par \par \uc2  Against the mark, the dollar was trading at 1.7665/75 marks, just above a session low of 1.7650 marks but down from late Thursday's 1.7775/85 marks. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/Swiss franc was at 1.4244/54, just above a session low of 1.4230 francs. It stood at 1.4398/408 late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "This sell-off in equities should see a shift towards safe-havens in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, especially for Swiss francs, sterling and marks," said Kusber. Further out, he sees a stronger German economy boosting the mark. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, the German Economics Ministry said it saw signs Germany's weak labour market was starting to turn and that the strength of its export market was beginning to feed into the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcj00r0n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
