\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo averts meltdown scenario but jury still out. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 1997\par 07:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 18 (Reuters) - Japan's abrupt shift in policy gear to boost its own economy while propping up the yen with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to ease fears of the world's worst nightmare -- a global meltdown triggered by Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But the jury is still out on whether Japanese authorities will take action bold enough to cure domestic woes and thereby provide significant aid to the troubled Asian region. \par \par \uc2  "It's a first step, but a very small step, in the right direction," said Mineko Sasaki-Smith, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto on Wednesday bowed to pressure to put fiscal reform on hold and rescue Japan's economy from recession with stimulus steps including an unexpected two trillion yen ($15.7 billion) income tax cut. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan took advantage of the surge in the yen sparked by the tax cut news to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively, selling dollars against the yen for the first time in over five years. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move knocked nearly six yen off the dollar's value at one point to below 126 yen from the 131.53 it touched that morning. The greenback was trading at around 127 yen after more apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Pressure from domestic circles and trade partners has been mounting for Hashimoto to ease his firm commitment to fiscal reform aimed at curbing the nation's big budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  Washington especially has long warned that Tokyo risked killing its recovery with a nine trillion yen ($70.8 billion) dose of fiscal rectitude in the business year begun last April. \par \par \uc2  Japan raised its consumption tax to five percent from three and abandoned two trillion yen of income tax rebates as part of a fiscal austerity programme to cut its budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  The pressure mounted as Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil worsened. And when South Korea's won joined the list of badly battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , worries grew abroad that Japan would join in a round of competitive regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations to keep exports -- its main area of economic strength -- afloat. \par \par \uc2  "The perception that Japanese authorities would let the yen slide in a beggar-thy-neighbour competitive devaluation of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very strong overseas," Sasaki-Smith said. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto got an earful when he met U.S. President Bill Clinton at an Asia-Pacific leaders summit in Canada last month and on Monday U.S. ambassador Thomas Foley again relayed American hopes for bold Japanese action to boost domestic growth and stabilise the bad loan-laden financial system. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto was also deluged at the start of the week with Southeast Asian pleas to help out by bolstering Japan's economy when he met leaders from the region in Kuala Lumpur on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In response, Hashimoto pledged at a news conference that day: "There will not be a world panic sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .` \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined with other policy steps -- including a plan to issue 10 trillion yen ($78.7 billion) in government bonds to the depleted deposit insurance scheme -- will likely dampen fears of a worst-case scenario starring Tokyo as the villain in a global economic meltdown. \par \par \uc2  "Japan stood up to be counted and said it would not let the yen go to 130-140 (to the dollar), that it was not going to allow a vicious spiral to take place," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  Worries persist, however, that the basic forces driving the yen's slide remain unaltered. \par \par \uc2  "Whether the package is sufficient to do anything more than stall the ultimate depreciation of the yen remains open to question," said Bill Belchere, head of Fixed Income and Economic Research at Merrill Lynch in Singapore. "We're holding with a forecast of hitting 140 over the next year." \par \par \uc2  How much help Asian economies can get from Japan also depends on the extent domestic growth -- and by extension imports -- are boosted by planned fiscal and financial steps. \par \par \uc2  Data released on Thursday showed Japanese imports slipped 4.2 percent in November, the first such drop in 43 months. \par \par \uc2  "Imports have been very, very weak because domestic demand hasn't strengthened," Jerram said. "This (new package) offers a slight promise of slightly better imports growth next year." \par \par \uc2  Hopes have been raised that Hashimoto's reluctant step away from fiscal austerity opens the door to further measures. "This is an incredible policy reversal," Jerram said. "It's not just the two trillion. There's more where that came from if needed." \par \par \uc2  But Japan must to do more than merely loosen its tight fiscal grip if it intends to help the rest of Asia export its way back to robust growth, Merrill Lynch's Belchere said. \par \par \uc2  "There's still a significant backlog of deregulation and market opening measures that need to be taken if they want to help the situation here," he said. ($1=127 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba00