\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Dlr Plunges After Japan Tax Cut News \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par 06:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-Dow Jones)--The surprise announcement Wednesday of a Y2.0 trillion special tax cut by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that came in the wake of the news caught dollar-bulls off guard, triggering panic-selling of dollars that sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest levels against the yen in a month. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also ended weaker against the Deutsche mark. \par \par \uc2  Most traders said Wednesday's action reflected a short-term response to the tax cut news and wouldn't alter economic fundamentals that suggest the dollar could move higher over the long term. \par \par \uc2  In a hastily called press conference Wednesday morning, Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced a special tax cut of Y2.0 trillion in the current fiscal year ending March 31, 1998. The step is partly 'to avoid a world depression originating in Japan.' \par \par \uc2  The prime minister's decision caught market players completely by surprise and sparked across-the-board selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen. \par \par \uc2  'The prime minister's decision was quite unexpected and also a de-facto repeal of his stringent budget policy,' said Kazuhito Furukawa, a trader at Nippon Credit Bank. 'If the Y2.0 trillion (tax cut) is not enough for a Japanese economic recovery, I assume Hashimoto is ready to do some more,' he noted. \par \par \uc2  Reports in the wake of the tax cut news that the Bank of Japan was selling dollars accelerated the free fall of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dragging it down temporarily by more than 5 yen to as low as Y125.87 at 0339GMT (10:39 p.m. EST Tuesday), its lowest level since November 18, when it hit a low of Y125.43. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar recovered a little ground in the Wednesday Asian session by Japanese buying on dips, and around 0520 GMT (1:20 a.m. EST), the dollar is quoted at Y127.50, sharply below Y130.80 late Tuesday in New York and down from Y130.81 late Tuesday in London. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also is at DM1.7726, lower than DM1.7805 late Tuesday in New York and below DM1.7819 in London Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-12-97 \par \par \uc2  0557GMT \par \par \uc2  1034 1863 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtch09psg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
