\par\par \b \uc2  INSTANT VIEW-Japan Nov customs-cleared trade data. \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 18 (Reuters) - Below are economists' initial reactions to Japan's November customs-cleared trade data released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  RICHARD JERRAM, CHIEF ECONOMIST, ING BARINGS SECURITIES \par \par \uc2  "The figure was close enough to the consensus that it's not going to have a big impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate... Quite interested to see the surplus with Europe doubling. That shows the yen is still very competitive against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. dollar, which is quite encouraging in terms of prospects of the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  "Most people are too busy looking at whether there is any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  going on in the market and not too worried about underlying trade trends. \par \par \uc2  "We think fair value for the yen/dollar is around 110, and now that the government has shown it will not allow the economy to go into recession there is much less need for outflows from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep on at current levels. \par \par \uc2  "So with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and statements of intent that they're not going to allow the yen to keep falling, effectively now the only question is how long does dollar stability around the 125-130 level persist and how long is it before the yen starts heading stronger." \par \par \uc2  "Given that the economy is going to pick up around March, April, that is when imports should begin to improve as well. \par \par \uc2  "It is important that Japan has basically said we're not going to see the external sector make the substantial contribution to growth as we've seen this year but we're going to rely on domestic demand to drive the economy forwards, and that does mean that the surplus stops going up. \par \par \uc2  SUSUMU KATO, CHIEF ECONOMIST, BZW SECURITIES JAPAN \par \par \uc2  "The trade account itself was within expectations but both export and import growth figures were not. No one was able to predict such a large drop of 4.2 percent for imports... Imports are in a slump due to the weak Japanese economy. The figure is unacceptable for the Japanese government which has been seeking domestic demand-led growth." \par \par \uc2  ANDREW SHIPLEY, CHIEF ECONOMIST, SCHRODERS JAPAN: \par \par \uc2  "I think the fundamentals still point to yen weakness for the foreseeable future. Exports are poised to slow, possibly quite sharply next year with the weakness in Asian demand and in addition the BOJ has given pretty much a clear signal that it has no intention of raising interest rates any time soon given the instability in the Japanese financial sector. \par \par \uc2  "This suggests that there will be continued outflows of capital as Japanese investors continue to go offshore in search of higher yields. So we don't see the recent yen rally as sustainable and we're continuing to look for a weaker yen with considerable risk on the downside for 1998. \par \par \uc2  On the outlook for trade friction with Europe, U.S.: \par \par \uc2  "There is of course strong political motivation for attempting to keep Japanese exports down on the part of overseas politicians. But...Japan has made tremendous progress in shifting progress offshore. \par \par \uc2  "Much of Japan's production for export now is extremely yen insensitive... For this reason we're not looking for a surge in exports even with the weaker yen and we think that fears of trade friction being fanned in the upcoming months remain overstated. And this is of course against the backdrop of a very strong U.S. economy. There is really not the compelling pressure on Americans right now to pressure Japan to lower its trade surplus." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtch00w7k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
