\par\par \b \uc2  Japan stocks, yen jump on surprise tax cut plan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masako Iijima \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Japanese stocks and the yen soared on Wednesday morning, lifted by euphoria over a surprise announcement by Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto that a hefty tax cut will be implemented to spur the stalled economy. \par \par \uc2  The prime minister said that a special income tax rebate of two trillion yen ($15.7 billion) will be included in an extra state budget for the year to next March, and that deficit-covering bonds may have to be issued to cover for the tax rebate. \par \par \uc2  "I have decided to carry out a special income tax cut as an emergency measure," Hashimoto told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The announcement caught traders by a surprise, triggering a steep rally in the benchmark Nikkei average of 225 blue-chip shares, while pushing the dollar down by nearly four yen to around 127.70 yen by Tokyo noon from its opening of 131.21. \par \par \uc2  "The tax rebate news came as a total surprise, and panic sales of dollars emerged briefly in the market," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Also knocking the dollar down was dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) in the Tokyo market at levels of around 129.00 yen, market sources said. No \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were immediately available for comment. \par \par \uc2  At the end of the two-hour session, the Nikkei average was up 513.89 points or 3.21 percent at 16,499.10. \par \par \uc2  The index was hoisted off a low of 15,795.20 to surge more than five percent at one point to reach 16,816.68 in reaction to news of the one-off cut in income tax aimed at jump-starting the country's lacklustre economy. \par \par \uc2  "The market was hoping for a special tax cut and the news really hit the spot," said Keiko Kondo, strategist at Merrill Lynch. "But this is only the start of the attempt to nurse the economy back to health and a continuation of plans to ensure a recovery will be needed." \par \par \uc2  The announcement came as calls intensified from the market and economists for the government to take decisive steps to encourage domestic consumption, a key locomotive for economic growth, which has been chilled by an unpopular rise in the national sales tax in April this year. \par \par \uc2  "This is the sort of news that the market has been waiting for," a trader at a medium-sized brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto said the income tax cuts, as well as tax reforms for 1998/99 and financial stabilisation measures decided by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP), will help clear uncertainties among businesses and consumers. \par \par \uc2  "I think these steps will make economic recovery trend stronger," the prime minister said. \par \par \uc2  The ruling party is due to announce its third batch of economic stimulants, which include tax reform proposals and measures to stabilise the financial system, later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The tax cut announcement meanwhile sent Japanese government bonds and euroyen futures tumbling by midday. \par \par \uc2  "The shock value of this was everything," one city bank trader said. But he and others agreed that the long-term impact on both the markets and the economy is likely to be limited. \par \par \uc2  The key March yen bond futures ended morning trade at its day's low of 129.05, down by 0.75 from Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  `Probably the financial markets regard it as a turnaround in the trend of fiscal contraction, which means there will be more," said Tetsufumi Yamakawa, director of economic research at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  "If we just think about a temporary two trillion yen tax rebate, the potential impact on the economy is fairly limited," he said, adding that consumers are more likely to save the money than to spend it. ($1=127 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Equities Desk +813 3432-9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtch00w4l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
