\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Dlr plunges, stocks rise on Japan tax cut. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masako Iijima \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par 08:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 17 (Reuters) - The dollar plunged nearly six yen on Wednesday, knocked down by surprise news of a Japanese income tax cut aimed at rescuing the economy and by persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly fell below 126 yen before recovering to trade at around 127.15 yen in late afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo share prices, meanwhile, ended the day sharply higher but off early highs as initial euphoria over a planned special income tax rebate faded. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto caught financial markets off guard with his morning announcement that a special income tax rebate of two trillion yen ($15.7 billion) would be included in an extra state budget for the year to next March, and that deficit-covering bonds might have to be issued to pay for the rebate. \par \par \uc2  "I have decided to carry out a special income tax cut as an emergency measure,` Hashimoto told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which opened at 131.21 yen, slid sharply on the news and was knocked down still further as talk spread that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place around midday and dealers said the BOJ sold the dollar intermittently at about 127.50 yen and below from midday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold more than one billion worth of dollars for yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day, its first such action since August 1992. \par \par \uc2  "We have taken decisive measures to cope with an excessive depreciation of the yen," senior Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara -- known as `Mr Yen" for his market-moving influence -- told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors then bought dollars on dips and U.S. funds went bargain-hunting, but market players remained cautious over the possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese and other monetary authorities if the greenback recovered to 130 yen. \par \par \uc2  The tax rebate announcement triggered a steep rally in the benchmark Nikkei average of 225 blue-chip shares, which surged more than five percent at one point to reach 16,816.68. \par \par \uc2  But market players appeared unconvinced that the tax cuts or other measures proposed by the ruling party would be enough to rescue the fragile economy from its slump. \par \par \uc2  "The market was hoping for a special tax cut and the news really hit the spot,` said Keiko Kondo, strategist at Merrill Lynch. "But this is only the start of the attempt to nurse the economy back to health and a continuation of plans to ensure a recovery will be needed.` \par \par \uc2  Calls have been growing from the market and Japan's trading partners in Asia and the West for Tokyo to take decisive steps to encourage domestic consumption, a key locomotive for economic growth, which has been chilled by an unpopular rise in the national sales tax in April this year. \par \par \uc2  "This is the sort of news that the market has been waiting for,` a trader at a medium-sized brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto said the income tax cuts, as well as tax reforms for 1998/99 and financial stabilisation measures decided by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP), would help clear uncertainties among businesses and consumers. \par \par \uc2  "I think these steps will make the economic recovery trend stronger,` the prime minister said. \par \par \uc2  The ruling party was due to announce its third batch of economic stimulants, which include tax reform proposals and measures to stabilise the financial system, later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The tax cut announcement, meanwhile, sent Japanese government bonds (JGBs) tumbling but they fought back to end the day weak but off earlier lows, with the benchmark March futures contract back above 129.00 on short-covering. \par \par \uc2  "The shock value of this was everything,` one city bank trader said. But he and others agreed that the long-term impact on both the markets and the economy is likely to be limited. \par \par \uc2  Economists warned the impact of the tax rebates would be limited. "Probably the financial markets regard it as a turnaround in the trend of fiscal contraction, which means there will be more,` said Tetsufumi Yamakawa, director of economic research at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  "If we just think about a temporary two trillion yen tax rebate, the potential impact on the economy is fairly limited,` he said, adding that consumers are more likely to save the money than to spend it. \par \par \uc2  ($1=127 yen) \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Equities Desk +813 3432-9404 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtch00w31\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
