\par\par \b \uc2  WORLD BONDS-JGBs to absorb supply but foreigners shy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Silvia Aloisi \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Measures by the Japanese authorities to boost the country's flagging economy are unlikely to depress government bonds for long, but foreign investors are still expected to shun the market, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Only a turnaround in the yen would make Japanese bonds attractive for overseas investors and any issue of deficit-covering bonds was likely to remain in the hands of domestic investors. \par \par \uc2  "You would have to be very bullish on the yen in order to compensate for the yield give-up that you take by investing in Japanese debt," said Adam Chester, Treasury economist at Halifax in London. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto surprised markets on Wednesday when he announced two trillion yen in special income tax cuts for the 1997/98 supplementary budget. The cuts are likely to be financed by a bond issue. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, a panel of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party also called for the government to contribute 10 trillion yen worth of bonds to the Deposit Insurance Corp to protect depositors and boost capital at troubled financial firms. \par \par \uc2  The tax cut was to be included in a third economic stimulus package, set to be finalised on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bonds slumped on the news with the March future closing down 60 basis points in Tokyo to 129.20. The yield on the 182nd benchmark bond rose to around 1.710 percent from Tuesday's close of 1.635 percent. \par \par \uc2  But analysts did not expect the weakness to last. \par \par \uc2  "In the very near term, the impact of extra supply is going to depress the market, but JGBs have good support and I don't think that we will see a major rise in yields," said Keith Edmonds, chief analyst at IBJ International in London. \par \par \uc2  David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber in London, said that if the new bond issue was intended to simply compensate for the tax cut, it could be easily absorbed by Japanese domestic investors. \par \par \uc2  "If it is in the region of two trillion yen, it is not actually going to overburden the JGBs market. This paper will be sold quite comfortably," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the economic package was not likely to fundamentally alter Japan's bleak economic picture. \par \par \uc2  "It's the third economic stimulus package this year, the other two have not had any material effect, the income tax cut amounts for only 0.4 percent of GDP," said Chester at Halifax. \par \par \uc2  Brickman said the measures announced were unlikely to boost Japan's growth by much more than 0.2 percent. "No more than half this extra money will be spent, maybe less, and this is a one-off tax cut, not a permanent one." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk would continue to keep foreign investors away from Japanese debt, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Yen strengthening overnight, helped by the first dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in five years from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was seen as a short-lived recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan may have had to stem the pace of decline in the yen, but over the medium term I still think we are going to see the dollar/yen moving towards 135 or even beyond," Brickman said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 126.93/99 yen at 1423 GMT, compared with 130.73 in Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Chester added the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely continue to flood the money market with liquidity, putting additional pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said with such meagre JGB yields, foreign investors were likely to put their money elsewhere, with U.S. Treasuries the top pick. ((International Bonds +44 171 542-6176 fax +44 171 542-5285, uk.governmentbonds.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtch00vlo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
