\par\par \b \uc2  Japan has dug deep into pocket for Asian bail-outs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 1997\par 15:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan, which pledged the most of any nation to recent Asian bail-out packages, has dipped into its foreign reserve holdings to avoid stirring up a political debate over such generosity abroad at a time of fiscal austerity at home. \par \par \uc2  Japan will, of course, have little difficulty finding the cash. It is the largest holder of foreign reserves in the world with about $228 billion. \par \par \uc2  And by using the reserves, managed under the Government Special \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Account, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoids contradicting Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's commitment to fiscal reform, since use of the money does not require parliamentary approval. \par \par \uc2  While it allows MOF to move quickly on aid, some analysts said such unchecked use of the special account posed problems. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the MOF can use the account freely at its own will is a problem," said Sayuri Kawamura, senior economist at the Japan Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  Any fresh government spending is likely to meet criticism at a time when Tokyo is striving for fiscal reform. \par \par \uc2  Last month, the parliament approved a fiscal reform bill which sets strict limits on budget expenditures for the next fiscal year and subsequent years through March 2001. For example, money for Tokyo's official development assistance will be slashed by 10 percent next fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Yet Japan pledged to provide $10 billion in financial support to South Korea this week to augment a financial assistance package by the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's offer is twice that of the second-largest offer of $5 billion by the United States, and comes on the heels of a similar promise by Japan in November to offer $5 billion to Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  In both cases, the funds, if needed, will be disbursed in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap transactions, in which Japan is expected to provide U.S. dollars in exchange for the recipient nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or its government bonds of equal value. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that the swap pledges posed a risk as Japan will be holding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indonesian rupiah and Korean won in its reserves, albeit temporarily. \par \par \uc2  Combined with its pledge for Indonesia, Japan will have an obligation to provide up to $15 billion from its external reserves through swap transactions if they are requested. \par \par \uc2  Although the funds, likely to be in the form of U.S. dollars, will be returned after the transaction period, there is a possibility that during that time the make-up of Japan's external reserves will be drastically altered. \par \par \uc2  Presently, most of Japan's external reserves are believed to be in dollars accumulated through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It is true that theoretically, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have such \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as rupiah and won) in its reserves temporarily, but the reserves will eventually be restored to the previous state since it is a swap transaction," one monetary source said. \par \par \uc2  Finance ministry officials defend Tokyo's support for both Jakarta and Seoul saying they are a "second line of defense" in addition to IMF's assistance and insisted it was unlikely to be used under "ordinary circumstances" in light of the IMF's support. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000