\par\par \b \uc2  Traders Await BOJ Yen Move To See If Words Turn To Deeds \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Casey \par \uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 1997\par 00:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (AP-Dow Jones)--Are Japanese officials just talking tough or will their \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the yen? \par \par \uc2  That's the question preoccupying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders as they mull the latest round of jawboning from officials seeking to stem the dollar's rise against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has climbed almost 7% in the past month, but this week its ascent stalled. After failing to hold above Y129.00, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at Y128.73 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buyer hesitancy was caused by concern over 'excessive yen weakness' expressed by both Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka and his more influential underling, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara. Both men vowed this week to take 'appropriate' steps to defend the yen. \par \par \uc2  But many traders share the opinion of Erste Bank chief dealer Stephen Flanagan, who said 'there is going to be a point in time when the words have no effect.' \par \par \uc2  The market, Flanagan said, in testing whether 'the words turn into deeds,' is inclined to run the dollar higher. \par \par \uc2  Besides, Japan's economy is in such poor shape that the yen can only go lower on fundamentals, many analysts argue. The fallout from Asia's economic crisis is yet to take hold and doomsayers are predicting a slew of fresh bank failures to follow the recent collapses of Hokkaido Takushoku Bank and Yamaichi Securities. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, traders are now clearly more wary of the Bank of Japan catching them short-yen and history suggests such caution isn't misplaced. \par \par \uc2  The yen is currently at its lowest levels since mid-1992, which was the last time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  officially acknowledged it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 03-12-97 \par \par \uc2  2329GMT \par \par \uc2  1143 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtc309bd4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
