\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Slight dlr retreat may not avert BoJ action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hugh Pain \par \uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar eased against the yen in Europe on Tuesday after Tokyo's big financial guns fired warning shots across the dollar's bows. \par \par \uc2  But it remained to be seen whether its marginal retreat would be enough to avert the first dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  since August 1992. \par \par \uc2  --------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab   MARKET PRICES AT 1647 GMT\par  Mark 1.7745/55\tab   Yen 128.72/77\tab   Sterling 1.6818/28\par  Gold $294.40/4.90 +0.29 (pvs PM fix)\tab  Brent $18.13 -0.03\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  FTSE 100 4977.6 +55.8  CAC 2913.09 -5.39   DAX 4072.96 +4.91\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which stood as high as 129.31 yen on Monday, retreated as far as 128.08 in early Europe but came back strongly to around 128.70 in late trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "When the appropriate time comes, we may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again," said Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for his ability to move exchange rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, especially in the yen against the dollar, were not favourable, and that Japan stood ready to act appropriately to ensure \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market stability.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was also threatened by a warning from an international monetary official, who spoke on condition of anonymity, that a further dollar gain against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would rebound on the United States.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The U.S. dollar's rise not only against the yen but also other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the rupiah would have a big impact on the United States because that would cause competitive devaluations," the official said outside meetings of Asian finance ministers in Kuala Lumpur.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Ignoring such comments is a dangerous game to play," said Nick Probert, head of spot trading at Bank of America. "The market is still fairly sanguine about it but it would be foolish to shrug off the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC Wood Gundy in London, said a possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had implications far wider than the dollar/yen rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What the authorities are keen to avoid is a situation where yen weakness feeds a stock market decline which increases pessimism in the economy," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Tokyo, dealers and analysts said Japan might try to maximise the impact of any dollar-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by timing it to coincide with big macroeconomic events.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These could include an agreement expected on Wednesday between South Korea and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) on a bailout plan or the announcement of economic steps by Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) expected on December 16, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets active and poised for fresh shocks, stock markets had a relatively quiet day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow, which surprised traders on Monday with a 190-point gain, eased at the opening by as much as 15 points but then headed back up to advance by around the same margin.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par London's FTSE, which had languished for most of the day, took heart from Wall Street's gain to finish up 55.8 points, 1.13 percent, near its day's highs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Frankfurt's DAX, which had started the day well, lost nearly all its gains to close only 0.12 percent up. In after-hours electronic trading the Xetra-DAX slid heavily but recovered on the Dow's firmness to end 29.52 points down, with little local news to influence it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market players said major moves by the Dow were unlikely today in view of the imminent arrival of significant pointers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan addresses the Economic Club of New York after the market closure tonight, October new home sales numbers are due on Wednesday and the November employment report follows on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday also brings the Bank of England's regular monetary policy committee meeting, which is expected to leave UK interest rates unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And on Thursday the Bundesbank's fortnightly council meeting is also seen ordering no change in rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Reuters poll of 10 economists also saw the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeping interest rates steady through to the end of 1997.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The few indications of inflationary pressure in the German economy were the chief reason they cited for the steady outlook.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bundesbank council member Reimut Jochimsen said he saw no signs of inflationary pressure in Germany, even though capacity utilisation in some sectors was becoming tight.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I have to say that I do not have any acute fears at the moment, but I always have latent fears," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bundesbank, like the Federal Reserve too, will keep an eye on Asian developments," said Marc Gero Daheim at Sal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Oppenheim in Cologne. He expects the Bundesbank to raise the repo rate by 20 basis points before next May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But the Bundesbank will not stab the Japanese in the back with an interest rate hike."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1997\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc201rv0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
