\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Hu/Rupiah -2: Not Part Of 'Second Line' Aid \b0\par\par\uc2 179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 1997\par 08:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (AP-Dow Jones)--Singapore Finance Minister Richard Hu said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Indonesian rupiah last month by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore was done on an 'ad hoc basis' and wasn't specifically part of the International Monetery Fund support package for the country. \par \par \uc2  Hu reiterated to reporters in Kuala Lumpur that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  'was not part of the second line of defense' that Singapore and other nations agreed to provide to Indonesia as part of that country's IMF package. Singapore pledged $5 billion toward the IMF package, which totals nearly $40 billion. \par \par \uc2  Hu said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , done by both Singapore and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was as 'ad hoc arrangement' and that future buying of the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also be done in that way. 'We will do it on an ad hoc basis,' said Hu. \par \par \uc2  He was among the finance ministers represented at a meeting of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations and other countries. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-12-97 \par \par \uc2  0709GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtc20997i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
