\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Traders/Dlr -2: Summers Tokyo Visit A Concern \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 1997\par 23:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Expectations of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it has come to be known, have in the past months seemed to cap the dollar's rise against the yen. On Sept. 24, comments on exchange levels by the vice minister of international affairs at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Eisuke Sakakibara, caused the dollar to fall about two yen to Y122.45. \par \par \uc2  But the lack of official response to dollar/yen levels as of late seems to have given traders confidence to take the dollar up. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they think that if the dollar breaks above the August 1992 high of Y127.50 next week, it could rise as high as Y130.00, despite the tumble late in New York on unconfirmed rumors that Japan may move to bail out its embattled banking sector. \par \par \uc2  'This just shows how nervous the market is,' said Deutsche Morgan Grenfell's Chandler. \par \par \uc2  But overall, said Dieter Bluhm, vice president at Wells Fargo Bank in San Francisco: 'We are going back to the fundamentals.' Bluhm said the dollar, which broke above Y125.00 for the first time in six month only days ago on Nov. 11, was playing catch up to the fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Henry Willmore of Barclay's Capital in New York said the dollar, according to fundamentals, should be trading as high as Y135.00 to Y140.00. Officially, Barclay's forecasts that the dollar will trade at Y128.00 in December and between Y130.00 and Y135.00 in the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  At 2218 GMT in New York the dollar is trading at Y125.40, down from its high of Y127.15 at 1449 GMT and from Y125.68 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the longer term optimism, traders were reluctant to be long in dollars going into the weekend, fearing a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ahead of Monday's meeting of Japanese Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka and U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Were traders nervous before the meeting? \par \par \uc2  'You bet they are,' said Barclay's Willmore. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-11-97 \par \par \uc2  2239GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011009dtbe0020t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
