\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar well-bid, but capped above 126 yen in Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0315 GMT\tab      TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0300 GMT\par  USD/JPY     125.85/95\tab \tab    126.02-125.60\par  USD/DEM     1.7266/71\tab \tab    1.7283-1.7222\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The dollar was well-bid against the yen by Tokyo midday on Friday, supported by firm purchases from Japanese securities firms and trust banks, but the yen's recovery in the cross trade capped the dollar above 126 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was weighed down by offers from Japan exporters as it approached 126 yen and large selling orders, detected at around 126.30 yen, limited active buying, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the dollar was trapped between bids by the Japanese and offers by foreigners, mainly U.S. operators," a U.S. bank dealer said. "As it failed to extend its gains overnight, position squaring could emerge before the weekend." \par \par \uc2  The mark was off its high against the yen as sales from U.S. fund operators emerged consistently on the German unit's rallies. The yen's slight recovery in the cross market weighed on the dollar/yen rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0320 GMT, the mark stood at 72.91/96 yen, compared with 73.07 yen in early Asian trade. The German unit closed at 72.86 yen in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Fear over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) was another factor in limiting active buying after dollar sentiment worsened following Thursday's rumour that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sold dollars against the yen in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the BOJ actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in any case, the market will still be very eager in testing the dollar upwards unless the BOJ gives a signal to the market by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly," a Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off comments by Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara, who spoke on Japan's "Big Bang" financial reforms at a symposium in Kyoto this morning. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said that the biggest problem in Japan's economy is financial institutions which have been impacted by the bursting of the economic bubble. \par \par \uc2  He said that Japan's economic fundamentals were still basically strong, and added that the role of the government was to regain overall credibility of its financial system. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Haruhiko Kuroda, director-general of the Finance Ministry's International Finance Bureau, said that Japan will act appropriately against excessive foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, but declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market refrained from trading actively as some operators were looking towards a second set of economic stimulus measures to be announced by the ruling Liberal Democratic Party later in the afternoon. But operators were not expecting any surprises from the package. \par \par \uc2  "People are bored even before seeing the actual economic stimulus package and some operators are using this as an excuse to not trade actively," the city bank dealer said. "The market is most likely to shrug off the news." \par \par \uc2  - Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-8785 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbe02k30\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoa