\par\par \b \uc2  Markets still doubt Japan will act to support yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Though fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen swirl through the markets, Japan's deep-rooted economic and financial problems still cast doubt on such action, dealers in Tokyo say. \par \par \uc2  These doubts could even lead the dollar higher, they say. \par \par \uc2  Rumours and reports swept through Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that the the BOJ had actually sold dollars against the yen. \par \par \uc2  But traders in Tokyo said there was little trace of any actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  having taken place. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar sales \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without fundamental support such as rises in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rates on expectations of economic recovery could prove to be in vain. \par \par \uc2  "If (the Bank of Japan) actually steps in to sell the dollar to smooth out its rally, that would give operators a good chance to buy dollars cheaply," said Kazunori Iida, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at trading firm Nissho Iwai Corp. \par \par \uc2  "Meanwhile, there's a big question mark about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will conduct large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  using its ample external reserves," he said, adding that such action could create a higher yen trend and increase the burden on Asian nations from the debts they owe to Japan. \par \par \uc2  The Mainichi Shimbun newspaper said on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been considering dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last week. But it said they have been unable to find right time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market due to falling share prices and poor economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  The fears clipped more than one yen from the dollar after it set a six-month high of 126.65 yen on Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  It was around 125.17 yen in late afternoon Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot sell dollars openly and aggressively due to reeling Tokyo share prices and fears that if they fall further it might undermine global stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Falls in Tokyo stocks would reduce capital positions held by Japanese banks and insurance companies, aggravating their balance sheets and intensifying worries about the health of financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "It would not be surprising if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place at any time once the stock market regained its stability," said Sadaaki Tsunakawa, vice president at Citibank NA. "But it can't do so under the current situation." \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, Finance Minister Hiroshi Mitsuzuka said an agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven industrial nations in Hong Kong was still intact, adding that Japan will deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues in an appropriate and timely manner. But dealers mostly ignored the remarks. \par \par \uc2  A G7 meeting in Hong Kong in September stressed the need to avoid excessive depreciation of key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would lead to reemergence of large external imbalances. Dealers said halting the dollar's rise was not a solution to the economic and financial problems that Japan faces. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise is seen as a by-product of Asian economic turmoil and worries about Japan's financial system, heightened by the failure of brokerage Sanyo Securities Co Ltd last week. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign banks in Tokyo were increasingly alarmed by risks of another failure of Japanese financial institutions, tightening their credit lines with Japanese institutions, foreign bankers said. This led to a rise in the Japan premium -- the extra price Japanese banks must pay foreign banks in raising funds overseas. \par \par \uc2  "The financial markets have been sending various warnings to the government, but it is turning a deaf ear to them," said Nissho Iwai's Iida. He said if the government did not take appropriate action now and things got worse, it would have to hammer out larger-scale economic stimulus measures later. \par \par \uc2  Noriyuki Takano, assistant director at Barclays Bank Plc in Tokyo, said: "A view that the dollar will reach 130 yen is growing." \par \par \uc2  "There is no way out of the current economic problems unless Japan finally carries out drastic structural reform including administrative reforms, rather than relying on short-term tax cuts or spending". \par \par \uc2  -- Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  email;Tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02lvu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
