\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Nikkei ends with pared losses as yen bounces. \b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Tokyo stock market's benchmark rebounded from steep losses to end marginally lower on Thursday, relieved by signs of stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the yen bounced against the dollar, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  But the stock market is still vulnerable ahead of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's (LDP) planned announcement on Friday of a second set of economic stimulus measures, which brokers did not expect to give effective support to the market. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average closed 6.9 points or 0.04 percent lower at 15,427.27. It briefly tumbled to 15,083.22, its lowest since July 6, 1995, before recovering. \par \par \uc2  December Nikkei futures gained 170 points to close at 15,470. \par \par \uc2  "The sell-off in stocks originally stemmed from Asian market turmoil, and signs of Asian nations cooperating to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped pull Japan out of the doldrums," said Hiroyuki Nakai, chief investment strategist at Nikko Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly fell close to 125.00 yen in Tokyo on Thursday, after trading above 126.00 yen earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's sharp decline sparked active short-covering in stocks, especially in the banking sector, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was hammered by talk that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 interventing\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell dollars for yen. A media report earlier said the BOJ sold dollars for yen in Singapore, although dealers said there were no traces of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah also recovered against the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Jakarta and Singapore, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stocks, already battered in recent weeks, had been further hurt by the yen's sharp slide against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Despite today's bounce, the stock market was jittery amid a lack of strong government measures to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We don't expect the government's package to trigger a rally in the stock market," Nikko's Nakai said. But he added that the Nikkei average is nearing a floor. \par \par \uc2  The LDP's second package is expected to be announced on Friday. The government will include some of the LDP proposals in its own package due out early next week. \par \par \uc2  "The fall in stocks is due to the hopeless mood surrounding the future of the economy. The falls shrink unrealised assets of banks, which in turn scares investors into selling even more," said Masaaki Higashida, a strategist at Nomura Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  On the first section of the Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE), turnover grew to 513 million shares from 499 million shares traded on Wednesday. Losers outnumbered winners 667 to 446, while 144 issues were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The broader-based TOPIX index also fell to its lowest in more than two years at one point in the morning, slumping to 1,177.73. It later bounced back and ended 1.39 point or 0.12 percent lower at 1,198.97. The Nikkei 300 inched up 0.07 point or 0.03 percent to 234.22 by the close. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said the next support for the Nikkei 225 average would be the 15,000 level. \par \par \uc2  After that, post-bubble lows such as the 1995 intra-day low of 14,295.90 and the 1992 low of 14,194 were seen as support levels, they said. \par \par \uc2  "One would certainly expect it to retest the lows of 1992," said Ken Okamura, a Dresdner Kleinwort Benson strategist. "There is a possibility of it going lower still if we do, for example, have a financial crisis." \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei has fallen about 25 percent from the year's high set in June. A large proportion of that loss has been racked up during the last two and a half weeks. \par \par \uc2  By sector, losers were led by real estate, miscellaneous manufacturing, air transport and credit/lease. Major gainers were communications, railway/bus and oil. \par \par \uc2  Yamaichi Securities Co Ltd dominated the list of most heavily traded issues and largest percentage losers. \par \par \uc2  After briefly hitting a record low of 114 yen, it ended down 25 yen or 16.89 percent at 123 in turnover of 28.368 million shares. Investors continued to sell the shares on speculation about the firm's financial health following the collapse of Sanyo Securities Co Ltd last week. \par \par \uc2  The second section index finished 13.12 points lower at 1,314.19, with 6.57 million shares traded. \par \par \uc2  The over-the-counter (OTC) index was down 8.82 points at 818.96. \par \par \uc2  - Hiroko Nakata, Tokyo Equities Desk (813) 3714-5473 email: tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02lpy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
