\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian stocks on rollercoaster after NY fall. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marguerite Nugent \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 06:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Asian stock markets went on a rollercoaster ride on Thursday after a sharp selloff in New York overnight further unnerved the region. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks extended Tuesday's declines amid continued concern over Japan's economy. Although the yen strengthened slightly against the dollar on rumours of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken further from its 0530 GMT level near 125 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the market recovered slightly in early afternoon trading, to be 0.08 percent, or 12.68 points, higher at 15,446.85, after closing the morning session 1.29 percent lower at 15,234.57. \par \par \uc2  "The market is trying to get its last two bits' worth in about how horribly the economy is doing, before the government draws up its economic stimulus package," said Harushige Kobayashi, deputy general manager at Yamaichi Securities. \par \par \uc2  The ruling Liberal Democratic Party is expected to announce on Friday an economic package on which the government is expected to base a final programme due out early next week. \par \par \uc2  However, traders say there is little optimism that the package will provide the stimulus needed. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, stocks dropped 0.89 percent at the opening in response to Wall Street's decline and continued to fall before recovering to end the morning session 2.16 percent, or 207.09 points, higher at 9,815.00. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the turnaround to an easing in interbank rates and a rally in futures, which touched off some bargain hunting. \par \par \uc2  "Having fallen so far, the reality is that there is a lot of value out there in the market," said Miles Remington, sales trader at SocGen-Crosby Securities. "I don't think it can go much lower." \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average shed 2.08 percent, or 157.41 points, to 7,401.32 on Wednesday after the Federal Reserve's key policy-setting group left interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Equity investors interpreted the Fed's decision as an indication the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more concerned about the impact Southeast Asia's financial turmoil would have on the U.S. economy and corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are awaiting Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony before Congress later on Thursday, where he will discuss the financial implications of the turmoil in Southeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  The negative mood caught on when trading resumed in Asia, dragging Australian and New Zealand shares lower. The New Zealand Stock Exchange 40 Cap Index finished off 2.01 percent, or 48.50 points, at 2,367.72. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Taiwan and the Philippines posted similar declines of 2.04 percent and 2.08 percent respectively at their early closings, and stocks in Indonesia and Malaysia were trading lower. \par \par \uc2  The drop in Thai stocks was limited by an encouraging report from the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  IMF managing director Michel Camdessus said on Thursday in Singapore that the country's implementation of a $17.2 billion economic assistance package had passed the IMF's first review. \par \par \uc2  He also said he had no plans to make changes to the IMF's assistance programme for Thailand. \par \par \uc2  Singapore stocks were little changed, falling 0.64 percent, or 10.85 points, to 1,674.94, while Korean shares gained 0.62 percent, or 3.20 points, to 520.69 at 0430 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was encouraged by parliament's passage of 13 financial reform bills, including one that required greater transparency of corporate financials. \par \par \uc2  South Korean shares took a sharper fall earlier in the session as hopes dimmed that the government's eagerly awaited market support measures would not be released this week. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02lmz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
