\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seen edging up, may test 122 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The dollar is likely to edge up against the yen on Tuesday due to renewed worries about Japan's financial institutions after Sanyo Securities filed for bankruptcy court protection on Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback may test 122 yen if it can clearly rise above 121.50 yen and 121.80 yen, although profit-taking sales by Japanese investors and sales by exporters may slow such gains, they said. \par \par \uc2  "I think that the dollar is likely to go up today, with the news of Sanyo raising worries about Japan's financial institutions," said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a trust bank said the dollar was believed to be on an upward trend again after falling from its recent high of around 123.05 yen. "The focus is on whether the dollar will be able to hold 122 yen when it reaches that level," he added. \par \par \uc2  He said that if Tokyo stocks fell on the Sanyo Securities' news, the dollar could be lifted up against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sanyo, a second-tier brokerage, was forced into court protection after nine top life insurance firms said on Friday they would not extend payment periods on subordinated loans. The filing was the first of its kind by a brokerage firm in post-war Japan. \par \par \uc2  The trust bank dealer said: "There won't be worries about a quick dollar fall and its support is seen at around 120.80 yen". Commercial dollar buying after a three-day weekend in Japan may also support the dollar, he added. \par \par \uc2  The trust bank dealer said Japanese life insurers and trust banks may continue to sell dollars in a bid to lock in profits on foreign securities, sales partly aimed at covering losses in their stock holdings. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese city bank dealer said renewed mark buying against the yen was also expected. \par \par \uc2  After dipping below 69.00 overnight, dealers found mark/yen too cheap to resist, scooping up the cross from around 69.15 in New York on Monday. It was at 69.90 yen at 2321 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday confirmed that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in concert with Indonesian and Singaporean monetary authorities to defend the Indonesia rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said such action would not have a direct impact on the dollar/yen trade in Tokyo, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  email:tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb303ehs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
