\par\par \b \uc2  Yen seen joined by Aussie and Kiwi as Asia victims. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1997\par 14:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The ailing yen is expected to remain on the defensive as a slump in Asian asset markets sparks concern about export prospects for the frail Japanese economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The latest turmoil will also heighten pressure on the Australian and New Zealand dollars as both countries have large export markets in the region, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The latest events in Asia have made it very clear that we are not dealing with a little local difficulty," said Robin Marshall, chief economist at Chase in London. "Among the G7 countries, Japan and Australia will be the biggest casualties." \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar bore the full brunt of investors' distaste of the region on Thursday, dropping to levels not seen since 1994 amid concern about Australia's exports. Asia takes over 50 percent of Australia's exports. \par \par \uc2  At 1130 GMT the Aussie stood at US$0.6999/04, down from a Sydney close of US$0.7076/81. The New Zealand dollar weakened further to US$0.6215/20 from US$0.6239/46. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment for the Australian dollar has soured significantly because of the Asian turmoil, and the New Zealand dollar is unlikely to escape selling pressure," said James Shugg, international economist at Westpac in London. \par \par \uc2  Shugg said, however, fears of a slowdown in the Australian economy were overdone. \par \par \uc2  "One has to look at the composition of exports," he said. "Australia exports a great deal of raw materials to the region, which should be less affected." \par \par \uc2  Yen weakness against the dollar was contained by concern about the performance of U.S. asset markets after the overnight tumble of Hong Kong stocks sparking widespread selling in other Asian and European bourses. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's blue chip Hang Seng index closed 10.4 percent lower on Thursday after plunging over 14 percent at one point as concern mounted that Hong Kong might be forced to abandon its peg to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also offered some support by the rebound staged by Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , led by gains in the Hong Kong and Singapore dollars. These two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  spiked up after short-term interest rates shot up sky-high as Hong Kong and Singapore authorities engineered a liquidity squeeze. \par \par \uc2  Opinion was split as to whether the dollar could top the near-five-year high of 127.46 yen it reached in May. Still, many analysts expect a slow grind higher towards 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The negative impact on Japanese competitiveness through the Asian crisis is adding to the negative outlook for the yen," said Nick Stamenkovic, economist at DKB International in London. "Fundamentally, the yen looks set to stay soft anyway because of the weakness of the domestic economy." \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 121.25/35 yen, compared with 120.60/70 in late Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to deploy verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's slide if the dollar approaches 125 yen but is likely to keep its powder dry before then, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to see the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the current situation," said Marshall at Chase. "They might have to allow the exchange rate to take up some of the adjustment needed from devaluations elsewhere in the region." \par \par \uc2  Rapid dollar gains will only be seen in case of a collapse in the Hong Kong dollar, which for now has been prevented by a rise in overnight rates to 250-280 percent, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a case of cash being king," at this point, Mike Grimble, investment strategist at Norwich Union in Norwich told RFTV. "The peg ought to stay." \par \par \uc2  But Paul Meggyesi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London, said pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to abate for long even though the Hong Kong dollar peg was difficult to break given Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve war chest of $88.1 billion. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said on Thursday the authority had sold U.S. dollars in the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's defence. \par \par \uc2  Still, "this story is set to run, even if the authorities have, for now, succeeded in pulling the Hong Kong dollar back from the brink," said Meggyesi at DMG. \par \par \uc2  Slower growth and deflation in Asia as a result of the financial markets' crisis in the region might dissuade the U.S. Federal Reserve from raising rates near-term. \par \par \uc2  While that should theoretically reduce the dollar's relative yield attractiveness, analysts expect the dollar to take its cue from a likely flow into U.S. Treasury bonds. \par \par \uc2  -- London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtan03o7c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
