\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ says mulling yen repo pact with central banks. \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 15 (Reuters) - A senior Bank of Japan (BOJ) official said on Wednesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering establishing a so-called yen repo agreement with world central banks that hold yen assets. \par \par \uc2  Takashi Anzai, executive director of the BOJ, told reporters: "We are considering a yen repo pact in order to make it easier for central banks to use the yen, which in turn will raise the liquidity of the yen." \par \par \uc2  He said the repo pact was not, however, designed to counter a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, although it might be used for that purpose as well. \par \par \uc2  He added that the amount of yen liquidity which the repo pact could provide had yet to be decided. \par \par \uc2  Anzai said the pact could raise global holdings of the yen, but the pact alone could not achieve the yen's globalisation, and a review of Japan's short-term credit market, such as financing bills, and of securities taxation were also necessary. \par \par \uc2  He said the repo pact idea was part of the BOJ's effort to bring the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system into line with global standards, and it was also in line with a BOJ plan to introduce RTGS (a real-time gross settlement system). \par \par \uc2  A repo pact gives participants instant access to cash by allowing them to sell their holdings of Japanese government securities to partners with a promise to buy them back later. \par \par \uc2  Japan has so far entered into dollar repo pacts with Australia, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and South Korea. \par \par \uc2  Anzai said several foreign central banks had expressed the view that the yen was hard to use and to hold, so he hoped the repo pact would raise the short-term liquidity of the yen, and thus help solve those problems. \par \par \uc2  He said a meeting of the 11-member Executives Meeting of East Asia Pacific Central Banks (EMEAP) in Manila on November 20 and 21 would discuss the repo pact, but the issue would not be taken up in conjunction with the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis or the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for its part expected to describe at the meeting past measures taken by the government to stabilise Japan's financial system. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he opposed the idea of establishing an Asia fund, an emergency war chest under consideration by Asian monetary authorities to help solve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crises, Anzai said: "If you pile up a significant amount of money, countries might overly depend on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without implementing necessary macroeconomic policies. I am just worried that such an idea might lead to the waste of financial resources." \par \par \uc2  - Tokyo Newsroom (813) 3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtaf04c1u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
