\par\par \b \uc2  German, Japanese comments blast dollar lower. \b0\par\par\uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1997\par 23:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 24 (Reuter) - The dollar fell sharply against the Japanese yen and German mark on Wednesday after top German and Japanese financial officials said they would work to stop the dollar from rising excessively against their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara emphasized a statement made by the world's industrial powers at a summit last Saturday in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told Jiji news service that the Group of Seven industrialized nations had sent a "strong message" to the markets by saying they sought to avoid excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves that could lead to trade imbalances. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara's comments were widely seen as a warning that the G7 would halt any sharp gains by the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The market just swarms like sharks when there's information like that to trade off. Sakakibara's comments seemed to have a big effect," said James Powers, vice president for corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Westdeutsche Landesbank Girozentrale. \par \par \uc2  The dollar late on Wednesday stood at 120.37 yen, down from 122.05 yen late on Tuesday, slid to 1.7735 marks from 1.7935 marks. \par \par \uc2  The British pound edged up to $1.6138 from $1.6135 late on Tuesday. The dollar fell to 1.4583 Swiss francs from 1.4705 Tuesday, and slid to Canadian $1.3882 from C$1.3906. \par \par \uc2  The dollar tumbled against mark after the publication of an interview with Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer in the German daily Flensburger Tageblatt. \par \par \uc2  The German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief was quoted as saying a falling mark would not be right for Germany, and that he would do all he could to ensure the mark did not depreciate against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Bundesbank is not going to allow the dollar to get above 1.80 marks for the rest of the year," said John Gariano, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo Bank New York. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar rebounded from its session lows during New York trading, and some market players expressed doubts that the Bundesbank or the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they came under pressure. \par \par \uc2  In addition, traders said Tietmeyer's comments appeared to have been embellished as they were translated into English, and that the original German quotes were far less inflammatory. \par \par \uc2  "The translation was much stronger," Powers said. "He said that if dollar strength became inflationary, they would act. But that's not the case at this point." \par \par \uc2  Some dealers also said they saw the Bank of France buying dollars against the mark in New York trade, casting doubts on Germany's ability to push the dollar lower. \par \par \uc2  France and other European nations have previously called for a stronger dollar, which would lower the prices of their goods in global markets, boosting their exports. \par \par \uc2  "German officials are quite happy with the dollar where it is. Our question is, 'Is Germany authorized to speak on behalf of those other nations?'" said John Hanley, chief dealer at Bank Austria in New York. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9o01z33\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
