\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex intervention \highlight\uc2  has limitations -- BOJ's Anzai. \b0\par\par\uc2 119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 19 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a country to prevent its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening has limitations because of limited external reserves, Bank of Japan (BOJ) executive director Takashi Anzai told a seminar on Friday. \par \par \uc2  By way of example, he noted that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar against the yen, there would be no limit to the amount of yen it could sell for dollars, because funds necessary to buy the dollars could be secured by issuing bills underwritten by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He added, however, that, "When a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under attack, there is a limited amount of external reserves." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt9j028yu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
